05:56.300 --> 05:59.060
But for trading, we focus Forex, I would say, yeah.

05:59.820 --> 06:01.160
Trading, we love Forex.

06:01.960 --> 06:03.620
Liquidity-wise is awesome.

06:03.620 --> 06:05.940
So we like that.

06:06.080 --> 06:12.020
I think as well what we found was with Market Cipher, if there's a lot of liquidity, the

06:12.020 --> 06:12.780
waves look cleaner.

06:13.420 --> 06:16.660
There's less, I guess like Kirsten likes to say, shark teeth.

06:18.100 --> 06:19.360
There's cleaner waves.

06:19.480 --> 06:20.320
There's rolling waves.

06:20.400 --> 06:21.400
It's a lot easier to read.

06:21.400 --> 06:24.920
But in terms of Market Cipher, we use it on everything.

06:25.220 --> 06:27.100
Like we have stocks, crypto.

06:27.420 --> 06:28.880
We love crypto as well, obviously.

06:29.360 --> 06:32.120
But from a trading day-to-day perspective, we love Forex.

06:33.220 --> 06:37.060
And why do you like Forex on like the intraday trading?

06:37.240 --> 06:42.240
Because I'm oftentimes an intraday trader with my BWAP strategy.

06:42.620 --> 06:44.900
So what about Forex makes it good for day trading?

06:46.120 --> 06:49.160
I think the best thing is that because there's sessions,

06:49.160 --> 06:52.040
I'm not saying that every other market doesn't have sessions,

06:52.340 --> 06:56.120
there's certain times in the day that there's more volume than others,

06:56.200 --> 06:58.560
London, New York, Asia session.

06:59.000 --> 07:03.080
And we find that because there's volume during those sessions,

07:03.180 --> 07:04.320
there's good setups, really.

07:04.720 --> 07:05.720
That's really all.

07:05.840 --> 07:07.580
And the patterns play out because there's liquidity.

07:08.640 --> 07:08.860
Yeah.

07:09.200 --> 07:10.740
London's quite good to us, usually.

07:11.520 --> 07:14.980
New York is midnight our time, which is unfortunate.

07:15.420 --> 07:18.080
But New York's also got some really good moves, actually.

07:18.420 --> 07:18.560
Yeah.

07:18.560 --> 07:22.420
And by liquidity, you mean the ease it is for people's orders to fill.

07:22.580 --> 07:23.620
But is there more to that?

07:23.760 --> 07:27.940
Like, do patterns actually play out more reliably and things like that?

07:28.060 --> 07:29.540
Like, what are the...

07:29.540 --> 07:30.960
Why is liquidity so important?

07:31.940 --> 07:32.460
Yeah, definitely.

07:32.800 --> 07:33.320
Like, I didn't...

07:33.320 --> 07:35.020
Yeah, go curse and cover that one if you want.

07:35.180 --> 07:35.860
Oh, sorry.

07:35.960 --> 07:36.700
No, I was just going to...

07:36.700 --> 07:37.300
Sorry to cut you off.

07:37.320 --> 07:38.040
But I was just going to say,

07:38.120 --> 07:41.980
I think the time to hit take profit is just so quick,

07:42.080 --> 07:43.140
especially off of New York.

07:43.260 --> 07:46.260
Like, you wouldn't have to hold a trade for long if you don't want to.

07:46.260 --> 07:48.960
Um, I mean, we prefer to swing trading anyway,

07:48.960 --> 07:51.000
but if you're going to do the day trading,

07:51.320 --> 07:54.980
like, you could hit such a big move so quickly off of New York.

07:55.440 --> 07:59.680
So, like, if you're the type of person who's uncomfortable holding a trade,

07:59.820 --> 08:01.500
I think that's the way to do it.

08:01.500 --> 08:03.020
So, yeah.

08:04.200 --> 08:04.560
Nice.

08:04.620 --> 08:05.140
I like that.

08:05.620 --> 08:10.780
Um, so do you want to walk us through a significant, uh, trade that you guys took recently?

08:11.580 --> 08:16.280
Um, how you used MarketCypher to get into it and explain the rationale?

08:16.280 --> 08:17.000
Yeah, I was...

08:17.000 --> 08:22.080
I've got this one set up on USDJPY because it's actually a call that we gave out on Sunday

08:22.080 --> 08:22.900
in the Discord.

08:23.440 --> 08:26.720
Um, and it's one that ended up playing out with the strategy that we're using.

08:26.880 --> 08:28.320
So, did you want me to break down that one?

08:29.060 --> 08:29.380
Sure.

08:30.460 --> 08:30.740
Yeah.

08:30.840 --> 08:31.080
Okay.

08:31.140 --> 08:35.340
So, I've got just the summary of the strategy just so people can see it and understand it.

08:35.400 --> 08:37.940
And then I'll show you on the actual candle how it plays out.

08:38.420 --> 08:40.520
But, um, essentially we'll just look at this for now.

08:41.040 --> 08:46.560
So, we're using MarketCypher B, the waves on the oversold and the overbought level,

08:46.920 --> 08:49.300
to catch the wick on a higher time frame candle.

08:49.980 --> 08:51.920
So, this is what it would look like, basically.

08:51.920 --> 08:55.880
If you're using on any of the lower time frame, paired up with a higher time frame,

08:56.460 --> 09:01.120
you want to catch this, is leading to the wick on that candle.

09:02.120 --> 09:07.120
And that's the benefit of catching that candle in such a fashion,

09:07.820 --> 09:11.380
is that your stop loss is probably going to be in the safest place that it can be,

09:12.080 --> 09:13.500
um, which should increase your win rate,

09:13.820 --> 09:17.320
and you're going to get the maximum return for that period of time.

09:17.900 --> 09:21.260
Obviously, you don't know how large that higher time frame candle is going to be when it closes,

09:21.500 --> 09:22.860
but, you know, that's not the point.

09:22.940 --> 09:24.240
You take what the market can give you.

09:25.000 --> 09:28.080
And that is what we're trying to achieve with our strategy.

09:28.480 --> 09:30.360
And we think it's very mechanical as well.

09:31.160 --> 09:35.360
So, I'll show you with the call that we gave out on USDJPY.

09:36.100 --> 09:39.640
I said that it was going to have a weekly one hour long this week,

09:39.700 --> 09:42.100
and that was on Sunday, I called it.

09:42.180 --> 09:44.280
So, on the 11th, I said that that's what was going to happen.

09:44.280 --> 09:48.700
So, I'm just going to mark up the open of the weekly time frame on USDJPY,

09:48.900 --> 09:52.200
and then I'll drop back down to the one hour time frame to show you the entry criteria.

09:52.720 --> 09:55.040
And that would be the mechanical entry criteria for it.

09:56.000 --> 10:01.340
And by mechanical, you just mean like step-by-step, replicable kind of process?

10:02.140 --> 10:06.640
Absolutely, because I see a lot of strategies where people rely a lot on judgment,

10:07.260 --> 10:08.880
which is fine if you're an experienced person,

10:08.880 --> 10:12.340
but for a beginner, I think that's going to be really difficult to replicate.

10:13.240 --> 10:17.980
You know, so, I think this would help people just to pull the trigger as the steps hit.

10:18.280 --> 10:19.800
You don't really have to think about it.

10:19.880 --> 10:22.300
Like, once those conditions are met, you get in.

10:23.380 --> 10:25.720
So, that is the weekly open.

10:26.620 --> 10:31.380
And then on the one hour time frame, you want price to be under the weekly open.

10:31.380 --> 10:36.820
And then using market cipher B, you want the wave to hit the oversold level.

10:37.200 --> 10:39.260
So, I'm using the negative 60 line for that.

10:40.060 --> 10:44.280
And once that happens, you want a body to close above the AEMA.

10:44.980 --> 10:46.400
And you can see it's not perfect.

10:46.820 --> 10:49.660
Sometimes you do, if you have a very tight stop loss in that,

10:49.720 --> 10:50.920
you're obviously going to get taken out.

10:51.660 --> 10:55.420
And it's usually a regular divergence that's going to stop you out.

10:55.800 --> 10:57.160
But we're aware of that.

10:57.200 --> 10:59.080
That's the only obstacle to the entry conditions.

10:59.080 --> 11:01.760
So, if you're aware that a regular divergence can stop you out,

11:01.980 --> 11:04.120
you could just wait for that and get in after that.

11:04.920 --> 11:06.220
And then that would be your entry.

11:07.120 --> 11:08.220
And that's it.

11:08.640 --> 11:09.920
That's your move for the week.

11:09.920 --> 11:10.020
I like that.

11:10.120 --> 11:13.660
So, you're using it weekly, almost like an anchoring bias,

11:13.800 --> 11:19.320
where you let the new week, the new weekly candle happen.

11:20.000 --> 11:23.280
And that way, you get a good price for the week off the road.

11:24.340 --> 11:24.500
Yeah.

11:25.040 --> 11:27.840
And I can show you, I'll just remove all of this.

11:27.840 --> 11:31.000
And I'm just going to take that entry.

11:31.200 --> 11:34.480
Like, this is the weekly approach.

11:34.680 --> 11:38.840
And with our Discord and our approach, there's multiple pair-ups.

11:38.980 --> 11:41.800
So, there's weekly one-hours, there's daily 15s, four-hour.

11:42.240 --> 11:43.560
Like, we have multiple pair-ups.

11:43.620 --> 11:44.860
So, we also have daily 15s.

11:44.900 --> 11:48.600
So, the daily candle is the 15-minute anchoring.

11:48.860 --> 11:49.060
Yeah.

11:49.400 --> 11:50.720
That's kind of our target as well.

11:50.720 --> 11:55.480
That's exactly how I showed you the illustration at the start to demonstrate it.

11:55.560 --> 12:00.000
Like, you're catching the wick on the high-time frame candle using MarketCypherby.

12:00.000 --> 12:09.800
I love that idea of using a big time frame candle as, like, a defense or a cover to protect yourself mentally.

12:09.860 --> 12:13.960
Not only mentally, but actually from the price action on the smaller time frames.

12:14.680 --> 12:15.080
Yeah.

12:15.080 --> 12:16.680
So, I like it.

12:16.780 --> 12:17.700
So, it looks good.

12:18.620 --> 12:18.840
Yeah.

12:18.900 --> 12:19.720
It's pretty straightforward.

12:19.920 --> 12:28.920
I mean, I guess, like, if you see, and how we like to talk about it, is on a weekly or daily trend, generally speaking, you're going to have multiple red or green candles.

12:29.380 --> 12:34.960
So, you're essentially just trying to catch those consecutive candles with the anchoring bias of the lower time frame.

12:37.040 --> 12:37.440
Yeah.

12:37.520 --> 12:38.360
It's so good, actually.

12:39.100 --> 12:39.660
So, what are...

12:39.660 --> 12:40.520
Because, I mean, like...

12:40.520 --> 12:40.920
Oh, sorry.

12:41.000 --> 12:41.260
Go on.

12:41.300 --> 12:41.500
Go on.

12:41.500 --> 12:42.840
Oh, no.

12:42.860 --> 12:49.540
I was just going to say, like, especially for that, like, the weekly one hour or the D15s, like, it reduces your screen time.

12:49.720 --> 12:55.440
I mean, you saw that one was nearly, like, a 5x play, and you only had to enter once this week, and you got, like, a 5x.

12:55.800 --> 13:00.000
I mean, some people are at the screen all day, like, for 12 hours, but they're not even getting that.

13:00.060 --> 13:00.580
Do you know what I mean?

13:01.000 --> 13:02.620
And that's, like, nothing against them.

13:02.660 --> 13:09.080
I'm not trying to put them down, but it's like, if you could free up your time and still hit that target, why wouldn't you just do it that way?

13:09.080 --> 13:11.080
It just seems so much healthier as well.

13:11.500 --> 13:15.840
Yeah, I would agree that, you know, whether you're a...

13:15.840 --> 13:26.680
Whether you call yourself a swing or a scalp trader, neither of those things demands that you're at the chart for 12 hours a day in that deleterious, bad, negative, like, bad health position.

13:27.400 --> 13:28.960
It's not a long-term strategy.

13:29.160 --> 13:31.620
And so, yeah, you have to learn how to optimize.

13:31.620 --> 13:46.960
And I might have some further questions for you guys, like, personally, after the call in regards to my strategy, because I might have something to learn from you guys in regards to how I can get my community to perform better using VWAP.

13:46.960 --> 13:50.300
So, a lot of this logic looks really cool to me.

13:51.120 --> 13:55.880
And I'm going to keep following your guys' channel, obviously, and learning with you guys.

13:56.060 --> 14:04.300
You know, that's why, you know, Market Cipher, we're different than most indicator companies because, you know, me and my brother, we're always...

14:04.300 --> 14:05.760
We're still learning every day.

14:05.760 --> 14:09.820
We don't come at people with, oh, we're smarter than you kind of attitude.

14:10.320 --> 14:13.180
And I feel like in this space, that's super rare.

14:14.300 --> 14:14.740
So...

14:14.740 --> 14:15.000
Yeah.

14:15.980 --> 14:18.480
But I had another question for you guys.

14:19.520 --> 14:28.160
What are, like, the key indicators of Market Cipher that you find most valuable to the mechanical trading process?

14:29.340 --> 14:30.780
And how do you apply them?

14:30.780 --> 14:37.560
So, I think, and this is my opinion, and Kirsten, you can jump in with your thoughts.

14:37.800 --> 14:41.760
Like, you can see with Kirsten's screen, we've removed a lot of the stuff off Market Cipher.

14:42.260 --> 14:43.340
So, we love the waves.

14:43.620 --> 14:44.840
We love the VWAP.

14:44.940 --> 14:45.880
We love the money flow.

14:46.140 --> 14:50.040
They're the kind of three key aspects of Market Cipher that we love.

14:50.160 --> 14:52.720
And the reason why is it's just easier to read.

14:52.720 --> 14:56.800
Like, the RSI's and the stock look awesome, but they're also so choppy.

14:56.800 --> 15:01.600
It's hard, it kind of muddies up the vision of the chart sometimes.

15:01.840 --> 15:04.540
So, we personally just use those three aspects of it.

15:05.560 --> 15:08.560
But, you know, in terms of the VWAP, I mean, the VWAP's super powerful.

15:08.920 --> 15:14.020
Like, you know, essentially, what we've seen is the VWAP is essentially just waves from higher timeframes.

15:14.020 --> 15:24.840
So, if you can catch those, it's a similar aspect and similar way of reading it from an anchor on a lower timeframe to a higher timeframe bias.

15:24.840 --> 15:27.400
I've noticed that, too.

15:27.700 --> 15:31.520
And by VWAP, they're talking about that yellow oscillator down there and the oscillator there.

15:31.620 --> 15:35.420
That's what we've traditionally called it throughout the years in the MC community.

15:36.320 --> 15:45.660
And I would agree that sometimes one of those crosses on a big timeframe, like this weekly chart, will basically do the work of what all the blue waves are doing.

15:45.660 --> 15:53.800
Because it's like an immediate timeframe directional kind of thing, like a short-term trend kind of signal.

15:54.600 --> 15:59.440
Whereas the blue waves are, I guess, more medium-term, depending on the timeframe.

16:00.200 --> 16:02.340
I'm glad you guys agree with that sentiment.

16:02.520 --> 16:10.300
And then the money flow is probably more long-term or more of like a leading indication, kind of telling you where things are, like where the volume and the money is going on the long-term.

16:10.300 --> 16:23.580
And, you know, I do use RSI myself, but, you know, we always made MarketCypher as just a grocery list of amazing tools to cherry-pick from.

16:24.060 --> 16:32.020
So, you know, you got every right to craft whatever edge that you wish with the software and it looks really clean.

16:32.320 --> 16:32.920
I like it.

16:33.740 --> 16:33.920
Yeah.

16:34.760 --> 16:36.200
No, I'm fully on the same page as Aaron.

16:36.200 --> 16:41.880
I mean, for me, it's all just the waves, as I showed you with my little illustration, my little drawing.

16:42.120 --> 16:43.780
So that's where it's at.

16:44.140 --> 16:45.420
And it works for me.

16:45.540 --> 16:46.800
And as you said, each to their own.

16:46.980 --> 16:52.720
So if you can customize a strategy based off of the items that work for you, then that's great.

16:54.840 --> 16:56.620
Yeah, we just wanted to see it, right?

16:57.540 --> 16:58.240
Yeah, yeah.

16:58.300 --> 17:01.460
I got a question for you, Aaron, in regards to risk management.

17:01.460 --> 17:06.840
Since she said that that was your specialty, how did that become your specialty?

17:07.260 --> 17:11.000
And what are, like, the top lessons with risk management that you want to share?

17:11.840 --> 17:12.780
Yeah, it's really interesting.

17:13.420 --> 17:17.620
You know, I kind of mentioned about my dad teaching me a lot of things as well.

17:18.460 --> 17:22.000
And he's a very risk-adverse person, I would say.

17:22.000 --> 17:27.740
And so, you know, in trading, you can't, sometimes being risk-averse isn't beneficial.

17:28.420 --> 17:36.160
You either don't pull the trigger, you can't cut your wins too early, you know, you're scared of losses, right?

17:36.580 --> 17:45.840
So it's really interesting about risk management and how I've learned it over time was it's really about mindset and your own personality.

17:45.840 --> 17:54.800
And that's kind of why when people say, oh, what's the best, like, in Forex, I'm not sure in crypto, but a lot of people talk about R, what's your R, what's your winning rate, all these kind of things.

17:55.960 --> 17:59.360
And I kind of think that it's not about that specifically.

17:59.360 --> 18:01.540
It's more about what you can take as a person.

18:01.540 --> 18:08.920
So, you know, when we start, when I started out as well, you know, like I was learning from a lot of different other people as well.

18:09.020 --> 18:12.400
And someone had like a 10 to 1 ratio.

18:12.820 --> 18:17.780
So, you know, they would lose nine times and win one, but that would still be profitable.

18:19.080 --> 18:25.400
And for me, personally, I wasn't, you know, mentally ready to lose nine in a row just to win one.

18:26.420 --> 18:29.760
So, you know, and a lot of people, to be honest, a lot of people aren't.

18:29.760 --> 18:37.380
But people, you know, that one winner will make them 30R or something crazy and they're successful.

18:38.020 --> 18:43.940
But I think it all comes down to your own psychology and your mindset and what you're willing to lose to win, right?

18:44.800 --> 18:50.420
So, in terms of that, like what I learned about myself and Kirsten as well was we're more inclined for a higher win rate.

18:51.140 --> 18:54.760
So, you rather win more times than you lose.

18:54.760 --> 19:02.060
So, therefore, you need to work out your percentage risk and your R to get to that stage.

19:03.020 --> 19:05.560
I mean, we're pretty generic compared to most people.

19:05.740 --> 19:08.300
You know, we risk 1 to 2% of our account on each trade.

19:08.640 --> 19:13.280
You know, we're trying to aim for, you know, 2, 2.5, 3R per trade.

19:13.860 --> 19:16.200
And over time, it just plays out.

19:16.200 --> 19:26.180
So, what elements of the strategy, like as you, you know, was the win rate something that was something that you had to work on?

19:26.280 --> 19:30.260
And were there breakthroughs that dramatically increased your win rates over time?

19:31.160 --> 19:31.360
Yeah.

19:31.680 --> 19:35.180
So, I think the biggest thing was, you know, it sounds really simple.

19:35.280 --> 19:41.500
But when you look at a daily or weekly chart and you see consecutive candles, the reality is you're just trying to catch those consecutive candles.

19:41.500 --> 19:44.240
So, the win rate is your bias, right?

19:44.400 --> 19:56.340
And then having a strategy and approach, which we've worked on together, which is those anchoring waves in Market Cipher B, allows you to increase your win rate as well because you're having the optimum entry.

19:56.740 --> 19:58.580
Similar to what Kirsten said, you're trying to catch the wick.

19:58.680 --> 19:59.800
So, we're not trying to catch the body.

19:59.880 --> 20:08.000
We're trying to catch the wick on the higher timeframe, which means it gives you the most space and safety to let that trade play out.

20:08.000 --> 20:08.400
Nice.

20:11.160 --> 20:11.840
I love that.

20:13.440 --> 20:14.500
Very good stuff.

20:14.840 --> 20:15.560
Very good stuff.

20:16.900 --> 20:17.040
Yeah.

20:17.140 --> 20:22.740
Risk management for me was tough because, you know, coming at this, you know, my background was not in trading.

20:22.880 --> 20:27.340
I was going to college for kinesiology, just doing random jobs on the side.

20:27.980 --> 20:31.820
Health and fitness was like my thing because in high school, I was never that fit.

20:31.820 --> 20:41.760
But the world of finance was new to me, even though I was a freedom lover and I loved cryptocurrency, the world of trading was new.

20:42.460 --> 20:48.760
And to see, to have a brother who was live trading massive positions, that was tough on me.

20:48.760 --> 21:06.900
Like my biggest hurdle with risk management was to not, you know, operate within the shadow of other people and to learn how to grow an account in and of itself, like in an isolated mind state mentality kind of environment.

21:06.900 --> 21:15.380
And that took years because, you know, I was in the discord with him and our technical analysis were blended and we would stream together.

21:15.380 --> 21:22.060
And it was hard for me to dissociate basically and like isolate my strategy from my brothers.

21:22.060 --> 21:29.280
But once I was able to do that, I was able to find success, particularly like the ETH bull run of 21.

21:31.280 --> 21:49.200
But even during those years, the success my brother and I had, him with his million dollar trades and me with moderate success, like in many ways, mentally, like I definitely paid for that because that marked a top in my trading career.

21:49.200 --> 22:00.740
Even though it was, those were successful years for me, that was like a price top for me in terms of my battle with risk management and trading in general.

22:01.360 --> 22:11.200
So I've had to come up again and, you know, learn to appreciate like, you know, everything that we accomplished, but also learn to start fresh.

22:11.200 --> 22:21.960
I think that with risk management, you know, losses are a part of the game and you come into trading super confident and you might have your biggest losses in your first two years of trading.

22:22.300 --> 22:25.160
And it's like, how do you grow back from that?

22:25.360 --> 22:30.820
You know, I think that's a big part of risk management and the mentality that I'm going to try to hit home this year.

22:30.820 --> 22:44.580
Um, so what do you guys have to say for someone who maybe, maybe, maybe they didn't bomb the whole account, but maybe they just lost like 20% and to them, it seems almost impossible to, to regrow.

22:44.880 --> 22:48.520
Um, how do you, how do you tackle a conundrum like that as a trader?

22:50.660 --> 22:51.060
Yeah.

22:53.060 --> 22:56.820
Well, as you said, Nick, it's probably like a two, three year journey.

22:56.820 --> 23:07.100
So, I mean, firstly, I don't know where that type of a person is in that journey, but you probably shouldn't start trading unless you're prepared to give up, you know, a few years of your life.

23:07.460 --> 23:10.280
Um, and, and that's just going to be the truth of it.

23:10.300 --> 23:11.540
And it's a very painful journey.

23:11.540 --> 23:13.700
Like I'm not going to sugarcoat that for anyone.

23:13.960 --> 23:21.020
Um, I think our, and I spoken about the root cause of pain to us is loss aversion.

23:21.020 --> 23:29.480
Um, and we've spoken about just because how human psychology is, I mean, every living creature wants to preserve themselves.

23:29.760 --> 23:30.600
Ultimately, that's it.

23:30.620 --> 23:30.840
Right.

23:30.900 --> 23:35.460
And the best way to preserve yourself is through like loss aversion.

23:36.120 --> 23:40.200
And I think if you understand that, that is the pain that you're feeling every time.

23:40.560 --> 23:41.940
There's nothing wrong with you.

23:42.080 --> 23:43.380
You haven't made a mistake.

23:43.660 --> 23:46.140
That is just how you are biologically hardwired.

23:46.140 --> 23:51.260
Like your body thinks that, you know, you're losing all these acorns, you're not going to survive the winter.

23:51.400 --> 23:52.320
You're going to starve to death.

23:52.840 --> 23:55.640
You just have to try and tell yourself like, you're okay.

23:56.120 --> 23:59.340
You know, this horrible feeling is there to try and...

23:59.340 --> 23:59.980
I'm a happy squirrel.

23:59.980 --> 24:01.340
Yeah, exactly.

24:02.120 --> 24:02.520
Exactly.

24:02.720 --> 24:03.660
You're going to be fine.

24:03.800 --> 24:09.760
And I guess that's why the risk management is important for that because position sizing needs to be smaller.

24:09.760 --> 24:18.760
If you're losing less per trade, as Aaron says, or if your win rate is higher, you're reducing that pain of loss aversion all the time.

24:18.920 --> 24:23.500
And that's going to help you get through that two, three-year journey, however long it's going to take.

24:24.540 --> 24:29.360
And that's like, I know Aaron said to you about the fitness side of it as well, the health side.

24:29.360 --> 24:35.940
Like, that's all super important because people can get lost in the rabbit hole and you really shouldn't.

24:36.060 --> 24:38.640
I can tell you, your health will decline.

24:39.100 --> 24:41.120
You're going to lose your relationships with people.

24:41.820 --> 24:43.780
You are going to lose, obviously, money.

24:43.980 --> 24:45.040
Like, that's the most obvious one.

24:45.140 --> 24:47.700
But you're going to lose so much if you get trapped.

24:47.720 --> 24:48.540
There's so much to that.

24:48.540 --> 24:48.760
Yeah.

24:48.880 --> 24:50.340
I've seen you guys on your Instagram.

24:51.260 --> 24:53.420
Are you shredded still, Kirsten?

24:53.500 --> 24:55.800
Or I'm pretty sure I saw some picture like, you were shredded.

24:56.080 --> 24:57.180
Like, are you an athlete or something?

24:57.180 --> 25:04.720
I used to be, man, 10 years ago, I had tuberculosis and it's like, I've been trying to come back from that, man.

25:04.740 --> 25:05.580
It was not easy.

25:05.920 --> 25:06.220
Well, yeah.

25:06.600 --> 25:10.900
That's something I noticed is a lot of our traders, they do have a strong fitness.

25:11.200 --> 25:14.840
Like, that's what marks a great trader is the discipline all around.

25:15.760 --> 25:18.700
And, yeah, like the comfortability of the pain.

25:19.120 --> 25:20.740
Yeah, the comfortability of the pain.

25:21.580 --> 25:24.540
And, you know, it hurts not just to lose but also to grow.

25:25.660 --> 25:26.100
Yes.

25:26.100 --> 25:35.560
You know, letting a good trade ride can be just as painful because you want to take that money off the table and you want to, it's just like, you know, double, triple texting that girl back that you really like.

25:35.720 --> 25:37.640
You know, so you want to take the profits off the table.

25:37.760 --> 25:39.780
It's hard to let a good trade ride, bro.

25:40.800 --> 25:41.040
True.

25:41.820 --> 25:42.140
Yeah.

25:42.940 --> 25:49.860
And to that point, Nick, like, it's interesting with what Kirsten was saying about, and you said about the coming back from a 20% drawdown.

25:49.860 --> 25:58.400
But, you know, and that point you made about fitness, it's like, how I like to think about and talk to a lot of people is like, literally, you just take day by day, week by week.

25:58.740 --> 26:06.120
Whereas if you only focus on that 20%, you're going to be like in a rabbit hole, staring at a screen, black eyes.

26:06.500 --> 26:10.600
Like, you just have to take day by day, journal day by day, journal each week.

26:10.600 --> 26:19.600
And that's why people are into fitness as well, I feel like it's like you're doing a bicep curl every day or every second day or every third day and slowly, incrementally, you're going to improve.

26:20.020 --> 26:22.720
So I think that's kind of the same mindset you have to have with training.

26:22.880 --> 26:23.820
You're just chipping away.

26:23.960 --> 26:25.080
You're slowly just chipping away.

26:25.160 --> 26:26.620
And it's just consistency over time.

26:28.480 --> 26:30.160
Yeah, you don't want to beat yourself up.

26:31.020 --> 26:35.760
Like, I've been using the anchored VWAP tool by Brian Shannon for a lot of my analysis.

26:35.760 --> 26:49.600
And he's got some great lessons to teach about anchoring biases is like, you can't put the anchor at your old total capital, because then you'll always have that bearing down on you've literally put a resistance overhead for your performance.

26:49.820 --> 27:00.860
Whereas if you can put an anchor at that low, like at the minus 20% value, from that point, you just have to hold a steady baseline and creep your way up.

27:00.860 --> 27:02.420
There isn't any resistance overhead.

27:02.420 --> 27:05.560
There's just some soft expectations to keep.

27:06.160 --> 27:13.560
And so that mindset, you know, this doesn't have to be this grueling journey through the gulag to regrow your account.

27:14.280 --> 27:16.480
This is all about, you know, starting fresh.

27:16.600 --> 27:23.140
Like, you're not a, I get that it's like everyone wants to become the dragon, but you got to be a chicken, then you got to be a pig, then you got to be a cow.

27:23.240 --> 27:25.700
You got to kind of take forms to get renewed.

27:26.360 --> 27:29.280
And, you know, one crazy year can undo a lot.

27:30.300 --> 27:30.700
Yes.

27:30.700 --> 27:39.780
A personal little story is my first year of trading, I lost five figures of settlement money that I got from surviving an accident.

27:40.000 --> 27:41.580
It was a near death accident I was in.

27:42.360 --> 27:44.860
And to lose that money was really hard on me.

27:44.980 --> 27:52.560
It took me about two years to like mentally, you know, get back and along with everything else I was dealing with.

27:52.560 --> 28:00.040
But in 2021, all that stuff went away, like the opportunities flooded in, the trades were going great, the coins were pumping.

28:00.700 --> 28:02.200
And next thing I knew, I was in profit.

28:02.200 --> 28:09.300
And like it, those, you know, those bad memories were covered over by a rapid flood of success.

28:09.300 --> 28:10.580
And that happens in the markets.

28:10.580 --> 28:16.600
You know, it's almost like the armies of the Lord rush in to redeem you or to save you or whatever.

28:16.720 --> 28:19.260
It's like the end of Lord of the Rings, you know what I'm saying?

28:19.260 --> 28:31.100
So, yeah, just keep, you know, set a new anchor and recognize that, you know, this account size that we're dealing with, this is you now.

28:31.680 --> 28:33.220
And it's only up from here.

28:33.280 --> 28:34.200
You got to have that mindset.

28:34.200 --> 28:41.260
I think the other thing is, well, when people get into trading, if it's all or nothing, it's really hard, to be honest.

28:41.860 --> 28:53.320
Like if you don't have, and this is how I think, if you don't have outside income initially when you start, it's really hard because you believe every trade is going to make you retire.

28:53.560 --> 28:55.980
You know, that's kind of how your mindset is when you start.

28:56.400 --> 29:02.420
But I think if you have outside income, you see it as a business and trading has to be kind of like a business in your mind.

29:02.420 --> 29:08.360
And there's wins, there's losses, there's a profit and a loss that you have to look at at the end of the year and say, how did I go?

29:08.520 --> 29:11.180
But at least you have other income to supplement that initially.

29:11.380 --> 29:14.380
And then eventually, of course, it can become your full-time job or full-time business.

29:15.980 --> 29:22.500
You know, I always tell my students, I tell them if they want to start their own trading business, talk to me.

29:22.500 --> 29:29.820
Because, you know, being a part of a trading business guarantees that you're here in the bear markets when things are cheap.

29:29.820 --> 29:34.220
It guarantees that you are still here tomorrow for your clients.

29:34.600 --> 29:36.260
You know, it guarantees a lot of things.

29:36.500 --> 29:40.580
It guarantees capital to trade with, you know, regardless of how you do in trading.

29:40.680 --> 29:46.960
That's a nice safety, a safe feeling to have as a trader is that, you know, backup plan.

29:46.960 --> 29:54.120
You know, you can't just, like, go back to waiting tables and stuff to, like, you know, I mean, you can.

29:54.240 --> 30:00.060
Actually, I would say that if you did bomb stuff, like, do whatever you can to work things back, you know.

30:00.600 --> 30:05.920
Because earning your way back is a great way to help your mentality, just like with that fitness stuff.

30:05.920 --> 30:10.640
Like, but all good stuff on risk management, guys.

30:10.720 --> 30:11.720
That was super valuable.

30:12.680 --> 30:13.120
Yeah.

30:13.920 --> 30:23.280
I guess one last question I'll ask you, too, is what advice would you give to other traders who are thinking about using MarketCypher?

30:23.380 --> 30:24.280
Like, they see the tool.

30:24.840 --> 30:25.800
It looks really cool.

30:25.800 --> 30:35.600
But, yeah, what advice would you give to them in regards to using, actually using MarketCypher in their broad, broader trading strategy?

30:36.540 --> 30:36.660
Yeah.

30:37.260 --> 30:44.200
Yeah, I think what I would say about MarketCypher is probably first to get your head around the divergences.

30:44.720 --> 30:51.420
I think if you're able to, and I know your brother does this, Nick, a lot where he scrunches his screen and stuff like that.

30:51.420 --> 30:53.520
And you can get, like, the full view, right?

30:54.220 --> 31:06.980
I think seeing, being able to see a regular divergence or a hidden divergence, firstly, I mean, if you're just playing those as well, just to start with and get used to how that moves the market, like, that is a huge help.

31:07.700 --> 31:11.560
So if they've got that lens, if they can scrunch their screen and zoom out and see that.

31:12.180 --> 31:16.720
And then after that, I would focus on the wave crossing zero as well.

31:16.720 --> 31:27.000
So if you know what your directional bias is, and if there's a divergence coming in, and if that wave is crossing zero, it's just carry on going with that momentum initially, you know?

31:27.460 --> 31:34.400
If we're talking about, like, for me, bare basics, if you want to start something simple, that's what my go-to would have been.

31:35.300 --> 31:35.420
Yeah.

31:35.420 --> 31:37.800
Yeah, same as me as well.

31:37.920 --> 31:46.720
I think, I think with MarketCypher, it gives you so many tools, which is awesome, but at the same time, when you start out, you want to make it simple.

31:46.720 --> 31:59.340
And you want to just focus on one or two small things, as Kirsten said, the divergences, or even just simply just a wave cross on a higher timeframe, that alone can be successful and repeatable.

31:59.900 --> 32:04.520
And I think the thing is, you have to realize as well, is that you just need to be simple at the start.

32:04.840 --> 32:09.800
And then, of course, you can increase your R on each trade or your win rate and everything like that.

32:09.800 --> 32:11.640
But you just need to simply follow a pattern.

32:12.400 --> 32:13.800
And that's the best way.

32:14.700 --> 32:15.180
Yeah, exactly.

32:15.300 --> 32:15.740
That's something.

32:15.740 --> 32:22.380
If you can replicate that, then you can move on to more complicated things, because you have to be able to repeat that.

32:22.860 --> 32:32.040
So, yeah, if you can do that and boost your morale with, like, those small little victories, and then move on to something more complicated, as Aran says.

32:32.360 --> 32:33.840
But that's how I would do it.

32:33.860 --> 32:36.240
Just very, very basic and simple to start with.

32:37.200 --> 32:45.160
I can tell you guys are experienced traders, because that's CF's approach as well, is to use the basic strategy to just murder.

32:45.160 --> 32:48.400
And, you know, stop making excuses and just keep it simple.

32:48.540 --> 32:51.860
Don't try to be a pencil neck and understand every little thing.

32:52.080 --> 32:55.680
Don't draw your divergences exactly from this point to that point.

32:55.680 --> 33:04.740
You know, my brother popped off because his TA is rough, and he certainly diverged from convention to reach success.

33:04.740 --> 33:06.640
Because what really matters, right?

33:06.720 --> 33:11.360
Is it the exactitude of your TA, or is it the actual execution on the exchange?

33:12.160 --> 33:13.040
That's what's more important.

33:13.580 --> 33:17.320
Abs are grown in the kitchen, not the work you do in the gym.

33:17.320 --> 33:20.740
And I would say the exchange is a lot like the kitchen, you know?

33:20.780 --> 33:21.000
Yeah.

33:21.120 --> 33:22.340
The charts are like the gym.

33:22.480 --> 33:23.960
It's kind of fun to be in the charts.

33:24.060 --> 33:25.120
It's fun to be in the gym.

33:26.000 --> 33:28.540
But, yeah, like, remember where the results lie.

33:29.300 --> 33:35.300
And be mindful of, you know, the pencil necks out there trying to get you on the technicalities and stuff.

33:35.980 --> 33:36.180
Yeah.

